# There are NO real machines in this repository: it is public, and a client's
# domains and stack list in a public repository are exactly the leak the whole
# split was made for. Machines live in their own private repositories.
#
# tests/machines/ holds SYNTHETIC fixtures: two configurations the engine is
# exercised on. Their .env files hold test values, but git is still no place for
# them: the example sits next to them, and confusing an example with a filled-in
# file is a bad habit anywhere.
tests/machines/*/.env
tests/machines/*/.env-stacks
tests/machines/*/stacks/*/.env
!tests/machines/*/.env.example
!tests/machines/*/.env-stacks.example
!tests/machines/*/stacks/*/.env.example
profiles/stacks/*/.env

# Machine state: everything that describes THIS machine and is generated rather
# than written by hand. Certificates, the ACME account key, the database list
# with passwords, the nginx includes. None of it belongs in git -- neither as a
# secret nor as a second source of truth.
tests/machines/*/state/*
!tests/machines/*/state/.keepit
tests/machines/*/dumps/*
!tests/machines/*/dumps/.keepit

# A platform that arrived in a fixture through bootstrap -- a duplicate of what
# sits next to it in platform/ and profiles/. The fixtures currently point into
# the working tree with symlinks (tests/machines/README.md), but a directory is
# possible if somebody runs bootstrap by hand.
tests/machines/*/.stackyard/
tests/machines/*/platform
tests/machines/*/profile
tests/machines/*/.vendor.lock
